   121	NOTES = r"""
   122	Definition and native objects.  Problem 1.1 and Section 1.1 define binary
   123	linear-code decoding and immediately rewrite it as fixed-weight syndrome
   124	decoding: for a full-rank (n-k)-by-n parity-check matrix H and syndrome s,
   125	find e with |e|=t and H e^T=s^T.  The module hands the solver exactly H (by
   126	its columns), s, and t.  The hexadecimal representation is only lossless bit
   127	packing of vectors in F_2^(n-k), not a reduction or discretised analogue.
   128	
   129	What produces the certificate.  A uniform full-rank binary matrix is obtained
   130	by rejection sampling uniform matrices until their exact F_2 rank is n-k.
   131	Independently, a uniform t-subset is sampled as the support of e.  The syndrome
   132	is the xor of those columns, so certificate construction costs t vector xors.
   133	No decoder, Fourier transform, collision search, or information-set search is
   134	run during generation.
   135	
   136	Hard and easy regimes.  Section 1.1 says random codes near the
   137	Gilbert--Varshamov relative distance delta_GV=h^{-1}(1-R) typically have only
   138	one decoding solution, whereas above it there are exponentially many.  It also
   139	states that known generic decoders become polynomial when
   140	(1-R)/2 <= tau <= (1+R)/2.  The non-demo presets use R=1/4 and
   141	tau approximately 0.2145=delta_GV(1/4), far below the polynomial band beginning
   142	at 0.375.  Section 4.1 gives a simple RLPN scale near 2^(k/2), and Propositions
   143	8--10 analyze exponential RLPN exponents at tau=delta_GV(R).  The paper's
   144	correctness analysis depends on Assumption 3.1; Conjecture 3.3 weakens that
   145	model without changing the asymptotic exponent.  This is therefore an
   146	average-distribution Track A benchmark, not a theorem that P differs from NP.
   147	
   148	Step-0 cost comparison.  At shipping size the exact Prange support-containment
   149	odds are C(144,41)/C(192,41), so even before the invertibility penalty the
   150	expected number of information sets is about 2^19.5.  Multiplying by the
   151	limiting probability that a random binary 144-by-144 matrix is invertible gives
   152	roughly 2.5 million trials.  The paper's simple low-rate RLPN discussion has
   153	scale 2^(k/2)=2^24.  There is no compact route that is shorter than decoding a
   154	uniform instance, so the family is not a Track B compression puzzle.
   155	
   156	Attacks.  The support is uniform and independent of H, so plant and decoy
   157	columns have exactly the same marginal distribution.  The outlier attack uses
   158	column Hamming weight, greedy repeatedly minimizes the residual Hamming weight,
   159	and random restart samples only legal t-subsets.  The domain attack is Prange
   160	information-set decoding: choose n-k columns, solve the exact binary system,
   161	and accept only a weight-t solution.  It is capped at 512 measured trials per
   162	seed; full RLPN/BJMM/May--Ozerov implementations are outside the standard
   163	library and are disclosed as README caveats rather than silently claimed run.
   164	
   165	Canonicalization.  Arbitrary coordinate renumbering permutes columns and
   166	carries support indices through the inverse permutation.  canonical_key sorts
