\subsection{Improving \cite{D86}  by puncturing as in  \cite{D89}}\label{subsec:D91}
 There is a simple way of improving the generation of dual codewords of low weight on  $\sN$. It consists in partitioning $\sN$ in two sets $\sN_1$ and $\sN_2$ with 
$\sN_2$ being a subset of positions of size just a little bit above $n-k$ (which is the dimension of the dual code $\CC^\perp$), say $n-k+\ell$ and then to use the collision method to get dual codewords of weight $w_2$ on $\sN_2$. The same method is used in the improvement \cite{D89} of the simple collision decoder \cite{D86} or in a slightly less efficient way in \cite{S88}. It just consists in finding codewords in $\CC^\perp$ which have weight $w_1$ on $\sN_1$ and $w_2$ on $\sN_2$ instead of simply weight $w$ on $\sN$. We have represented in Figure \ref{fig:Dumer1} the form of the parity-checks we produce with this method. Note that the weight $w_1$ is expected to be half the size $k-\ell-s$ of $\sN_1$.
 
