codex
The audit found one substantive issue in the draft: it counted verification of an already-known support as the “intended route,” while G9(c) asks for the solver’s post-insight recovery route. Random GV decoding has no sub-300-operation route, so that version would measure infeasible search rather than insight. I’m switching to the paper’s own LPN/Fourier object as a Track B family: generic maximum-likelihood recovery takes millions of exact steps, while a hidden degree-two evaluation symmetry yields a short exact recovery if noticed.
The existing Track A draft mismeasured G9(c) by counting only witness verification. I am redesigning it around Section 3's native LPN maximum-likelihood object so the mechanical algorithm is large but the post-insight route is genuinely under 300 operations (Track B).
