 This module turns Carrier, Debris-Alazard, Meyer-Hilfiger, and Tillich’s
 [*Statistical Decoding 2.0: Reducing Decoding to LPN*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.02201)
-into exact witness problems.  The solver receives a uniformly sampled
-full-rank binary parity-check matrix `H`, a syndrome `s`, and a weight `t`.
-They must return the support of any weight-`t` error `e` satisfying
-`H e^T = s^T`.  Columns are losslessly packed as hexadecimal binary vectors;
-checking an answer is just exact range/weight validation and xor.
+into exact witness problems. The solver receives labeled samples `(x,a,b)` over
+`F_2` and an exact noise weight, and must return any nonzero secret `z` for which
+exactly that many equations `b=<z,a>` disagree. Verification reconstructs all
+predicted bits with integer xor and compares one exact Hamming weight. The
+generator samples `z` first, builds the responses around it, and never recovers
+the answer by solving its own instance.
 
-The answer is known by inverse generation: sample `H`, sample the support,
-then xor its columns to obtain `s`.  No decoder is run while building an
-instance.  This is exactly Problem 1.1 and its syndrome formulation in
-Section 1.1, not a graph surrogate or a convenience reduction.
+## Why Track B
 
-## Why Track A
-
-The non-demo presets use random rate-`1/4` codes and
-`t/n` close to `delta_GV(1/4) ≈ 0.2145`.  Section 1.1 identifies this
-Gilbert–Varshamov regime as the point where a conditioned random instance
-typically has only one solution.  It also identifies the easy regimes that
-the generator avoids: above GV there are exponentially many answers, and
-known decoders become polynomial for
-`(1-R)/2 <= t/n <= (1+R)/2` (starting at `0.375` here).
+Problem 1.2 defines LPN, while Sections 3.1–3.2 turn restricted dual checks into
+LPN samples and prove that the Walsh coefficient at `z` is `N-2d`, where `d` is
+the response distance. Thus maximum likelihood is a Walsh–Hadamard transform in
+`O(s 2^s)`. Shipping has secret dimension `s=21`, so that route costs
+`21*2^21 = 44,040,192` scalar additions. The stronger bit-packed exact scan
+actually run here succeeded on 8/8 instances, visiting a median 1,435,390
+candidates (4,306,170 word operations) in 0.333 seconds. It is easy for a
+computer and not executable unaided in context—the defining Track B gap.
 
-Known generic recovery remains exponential.  Section 4.1 gives the simple
-low-rate RLPN scale near `2^(k/2)`; Propositions 4.4 and 5.1 give the
-asymptotic complexity exponents of stronger collision-based variants at
-`tau=delta_GV(R)`, conditional on Conjecture 3.11.  At shipping size, support containment for classical
-Prange ISD alone costs an expected
-`C(192,41)/C(144,41) = 734,176.6` trials.  Accounting for the limiting
-`0.288788` invertibility probability gives about `2,542,267` trials.  The
-paper’s simple RLPN scale is `2^24 = 16,777,216` core units.  There is no
-short compact route through a uniformly random matrix, so this is not a
-Track B compression puzzle.
+The compact route is different. Feature coordinates secretly permute all
+nonconstant degree-at-most-two Boolean monomials in six variables. Singleton
+labels isolate the six linear features. For every pair `{i,j}`, xor its feature
+row with the two singleton rows to isolate one quadratic feature; the same xor
+of response bits gives its coefficient. All noise labels have Hamming weight at
+least three, so those equations are exact. Fifteen pairs times four xors is a
+verified 60-operation route.
 
-The paper is candid about a modeling limitation: Algorithm 3.1’s analysis
-uses Assumption 3.7, and Section 3.4 replaces it experimentally with
-Conjecture 3.11.  This benchmark relies on the established random syndrome
-decoding distribution, not on that assumption being a proved theorem.
+The sample code is a zero-constant subcode of `RM(2,m)`, whose minimum distance
+is at least `2^(m-2)`. Noise is `2^(m-3)-1`, strictly below half that distance,
+so the planted secret is unique. Section 1.1’s generic easy cases were also
+kept out of the Track A draft: decoding becomes polynomial in its high-noise
+band and additional solutions proliferate above the Gilbert–Varshamov distance.
+Here the relevant easy cases are zero noise with an exposed basis, or revealing
+the hidden monomial coordinate map; only the demo deliberately uses zero noise.
 
